#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2efe9f75-18c0-5a86-b56b-3f338b7e5c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1702" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the graph of the straight line below?\n\nI. $y=2x-1$\n\nII. $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$\n\nIII. $2x-y=1$\n\n![](images/93/93dc9d447c15e4597549996b1f0b533cf86783b5cc5df032a4c46d4ca8934abc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/a9/a9f18453dedb1763908825301f80ef86307d3a3aceaf678bbe8579a42a0996f5.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=2x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =2(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $2x-y=1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-(-1) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓ \\\\ 2(2)-(3) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2x-y=1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=2x-1$ and $2x-y=1,$ corresponding to answer choices I and III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501336 :question-generator/id #uuid "b429cfa3-b141-58ec-8aa2-78825cd36898" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186054802.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edec9d12-0bd4-51b1-857c-39b0d3a639e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439845} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4130b8-9669-55b8-8101-3fcb6c6ada16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edec9d12-0bd4-51b1-857c-39b0d3a639e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439845} :answer-field/id #uuid "69a5e73f-3177-5c10-a5a9-440d3c78400f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb663d8-de5a-51e3-bb4d-8e4f975e7db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439847} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0202b1-3256-56a6-9f81-b5d0622b9978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f0202b1-3256-56a6-9f81-b5d0622b9978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439848} :answer-field/id #uuid "49e99a0a-f180-5be2-8fc7-1134f81777c9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27364161-b9ad-5a6e-b527-4b3e2d5b8883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439849} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e1b9e7-60c6-56f9-aca1-3d50f7fe497b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27364161-b9ad-5a6e-b527-4b3e2d5b8883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439849} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ff5166-67c3-5684-8f52-2c346e801f1d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440282}] :question/id #uuid "56e71e28-d3a5-545f-bf8d-30361fbf20ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-54285" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce176ba6bb73da8d6af410bedad4733cf3e705c70e28f133d771b89f78130c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y = x + \\frac{1}{2}$\n- **II.** $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$\n- **III.** $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The line crosses the y-axis at 2 and passes through (4,4); its slope is 1/2. Therefore y=x/2+2, statement II."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acf10a5d-ded4-5b84-b33b-6fd30a508b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439851} {:answer/id #uuid "136aebe2-1e23-5e43-bca6-1706231d0c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf10a5d-ded4-5b84-b33b-6fd30a508b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439851} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bfae510-b96d-5e86-9727-d50c4fd631a3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c172c4a-e1a8-56f7-9795-82510cd46049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439853} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1a8025-4883-5f71-a62a-efae12144e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c172c4a-e1a8-56f7-9795-82510cd46049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439853} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeab6991-adc5-5ace-ba4f-ecc5f05657e8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440284} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aba6425-8dab-5304-9842-8e92e1722604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439855} {:answer/id #uuid "48a91d43-b10e-5fa5-ac58-14ddb60ffc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a91d43-b10e-5fa5-ac58-14ddb60ffc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439856} :answer-field/id #uuid "415c96ce-c78f-50f0-9f80-e3fc5b279dba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440285}] :question/id #uuid "2fe51fc5-fce7-5fb6-911c-f561e72ff3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-59732" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d78eaaedd26a27d9163fdcddfce5c5ed429e441a75d361f0840935fce9235c6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x + 2y = 4$\n- **II.** $y = 2 - x$\n- **III.** $y = 4 - x$" :question/worked-solution "The line has intercepts (0,4) and (4,0), so its equation is y=4-x, statement III."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce4d23e-eb28-56bb-bb93-3733846a6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489585} {:answer/id #uuid "59fbb6ed-802e-5fc7-981b-ad071fe8f05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489735} {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d8e2ce-cf6e-51e6-a3d1-b415eae5cfb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489933} {:answer/id #uuid "e108ba0b-b8a5-543a-a12b-2f22efb52a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} :answer-field/id #uuid "8571c77b-dc56-5521-b8ff-cf319f34e7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d3bda62-1eef-5cb9-886b-004877c9a204" :question/math-academy-id "q-71837" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb1b55-d961-5760-970a-55e90ab17c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489858} {:answer/id #uuid "c834e69d-11f0-589f-aef2-acdc449ab184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489948} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1f968-d792-57b5-9f02-cbac084ac726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489956} {:answer/id #uuid "e524c8b2-8988-581d-b06e-10a171578329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f33cfa-5885-5a2f-90fc-d69e3d361204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a635937b-674b-54dd-a770-48fe7b870be8" :question/math-academy-id "q-54293" :question/problem "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3da477-2f8c-5b2b-8534-55a21ba63377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501316} {:answer/id #uuid "25f25fe2-2d7e-54ee-967f-6bb9248a110b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501319} {:answer/id #uuid "277c7b29-7430-521f-9d76-62c8fd5674b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501320} {:answer/id #uuid "92cee00b-fc75-54a9-b753-d48fe2943e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501325} {:answer/id #uuid "bae18ee6-b3b9-5327-ad96-6d55d7e27bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3da477-2f8c-5b2b-8534-55a21ba63377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501316} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1108d84-a4e2-5397-a2f7-9adbc9f6a9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61c31858-4aad-578b-a21e-e968c5ca8b40" :question/problem "![](images/76/7688f7536a66b7a737d58031b9b8752aabaa84a914ff5deb799e7060d7a90aeb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(6,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/cb/cbec3e69d5f8a5bbcfc7d97d95c9f3d3ec93be63b1c8de2ceba3e3a24a9dba13.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(6,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(6)+4 \\\\ 0 & =-4+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(6,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{2}{3}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{2}{3}(6)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4+4 \\\\ 0 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015abcb0-80a5-5dc1-b79f-79d7198d8b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501315} {:answer/id #uuid "1d75fe37-e4ce-5bc4-98f8-d2fb16b56012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501318} {:answer/id #uuid "7d397adc-2e01-5cd8-a945-f4855a2fd4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501324} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3194ba-e75e-59ea-b78e-7f3fe9b442f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501327} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c58012-7269-5610-aef9-dbc7d838d8f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6c58012-7269-5610-aef9-dbc7d838d8f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501333} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a010cc-cba0-5af2-91cb-dae10509e3db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b3d6f732-ed9f-51fe-87bd-f9f387742c79" :question/problem "![](images/87/87cbbd5a53079bb2cd744e1af18e901f6e54239a0fc6330984659c75cbdd3509.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=9$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,9)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/c3/c3a0113d32377bd9bac1569081dce1caad04e1cd385805fbb225c3964e7f85d7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=9.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9)+3(0) & =9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(3) & =9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=9$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(9)+(0) & =3 \\\\ 3+0 & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(3) & =3 \\\\ 0+3 & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+9.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,9)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9) & =-3(0)+9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(3)+9 \\\\ 0 & =-9+9 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+9$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116550ec-7c28-5e18-9fdd-f2d1f181ab88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501317} {:answer/id #uuid "4566a58e-dd27-57f2-8c17-67e13d84f401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501321} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9607e4-f2bb-5686-8bd2-b386551546bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501326} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e72227-20c4-5321-a16a-1705dfdcf169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501328} {:answer/id #uuid "cf447eac-1836-54a3-8082-5758760bcbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf447eac-1836-54a3-8082-5758760bcbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5912bad9-fd12-5d7e-8bfc-20dae374c49f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff7154d8-1178-59c1-89cf-d6727039f41b" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0218ba02ef7305b1f22582379d15e8da24c859a239b45130c053aa5c3f8cde1.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-3x+\\frac{3}{8}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{3}{8}x+3$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,3)$ and $(8,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/f1/f193f5ea70aa459427c5c4abecfdb74c3e12c6de4e22522d71f5ca1f6b1b9cc1.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-3x+\\frac{3}{8}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,3)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3) & =-3(0)+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+\\frac{3}{8}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{3}{8}x+3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,3)$ and $B(8,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(0)+3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(8)+3 \\\\ 0 & =-3+3 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{3}{8}x+3$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,3)$ and $B(8,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3) & =\\frac{3}{8}(0)+3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{3}{8}(8)+3 \\\\ 0 & =3+3 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{3}{8}x+3,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}]}
